1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,369
In May, three astronauts flew up to China's Tiangong space station,

2
00:00:04,369 --> 00:00:06,920
for what was meant to be a six-month stay.

3
00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:11,837
But China's space agency says one of them won't come home with the others.

4
00:00:11,837 --> 00:00:14,702
That astronaut will stay for about a whole year.

5
00:00:14,702 --> 00:00:15,538
China's first.

6
00:00:16,038 --> 00:00:19,373
So what if you stayed up there, not for one year, but for ten?

7
00:00:23,633 --> 00:00:25,994
First, what's actually happening.

8
00:00:25,994 --> 00:00:29,928
Shenzhou-23 launched on the twenty-fourth of May, 2026,

9
00:00:29,928 --> 00:00:33,362
with commander Zhu Yangzhu, pilot Zhang Zhiyuan,

10
00:00:33,362 --> 00:00:35,866
and payload specialist Lai Ka-ying,

11
00:00:35,866 --> 00:00:39,299
the first person from Hong Kong to fly to space.

12
00:00:39,799 --> 00:00:41,123
The day before launch,

13
00:00:41,123 --> 00:00:46,477
the China Manned Space Agency said one of the three would stay in orbit for about a year,

14
00:00:46,477 --> 00:00:49,725
to study what long spaceflight does to the human body.

15
00:00:50,225 --> 00:00:51,178
Which astronaut?

16
00:00:51,178 --> 00:00:52,785
That hasn't been announced.

17
00:00:52,785 --> 00:00:56,296
The agency says the choice depends on how the mission goes.

18
00:00:56,796 --> 00:00:59,355
Meanwhile, the next ship, Shenzhou-24,

19
00:00:59,355 --> 00:01:03,664
was rolled out to its launch pad on the seventeenth of November.

20
00:01:03,664 --> 00:01:08,309
It's expected to carry the first foreign astronaut to visit Tiangong,

21
00:01:08,309 --> 00:01:10,464
from Pakistan, for about a week.

22
00:01:10,464 --> 00:01:13,628
As this video was made, it hadn't launched yet.

23
00:01:14,467 --> 00:01:16,536
Now, the rules of our what-if.

24
00:01:16,536 --> 00:01:19,087
You move into a station in low orbit,

25
00:01:19,087 --> 00:01:22,467
like Tiangong or the International Space Station.

26
00:01:22,467 --> 00:01:24,950
You get food, air, water, and a gym.

27
00:01:25,450 --> 00:01:26,679
You never come down.

28
00:01:26,679 --> 00:01:28,155
Not for ten whole years.

29
00:01:28,655 --> 00:01:33,341
And a station like this races around the Earth about every ninety minutes.

30
00:01:33,341 --> 00:01:36,760
That's sixteen sunrises and sixteen sunsets every day.

31
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:41,067
By our rough math, ten years is about fifty-eight thousand sunrises.

32
00:01:41,567 --> 00:01:45,454
Every single day, you cover about the distance to the Moon and back.

33
00:01:45,454 --> 00:01:47,798
And your body has to deal with all of it.

34
00:01:50,533 --> 00:01:52,685
The engines cut off, and you float.

35
00:01:52,685 --> 00:01:56,435
Within hours, your body notices that something is very wrong.

36
00:01:56,935 --> 00:02:01,420
On Earth, gravity pulls your blood and other fluids down toward your feet.

37
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:02,753
Up here, nothing does.

38
00:02:02,753 --> 00:02:05,298
So about two liters of fluid shift upward,

39
00:02:05,298 --> 00:02:08,085
out of your legs and into your chest and head.

40
00:02:08,585 --> 00:02:09,822
Your face puffs up.

41
00:02:09,822 --> 00:02:11,189
Your legs get skinny.

42
00:02:11,189 --> 00:02:13,141
Astronauts have a name for it:

43
00:02:13,141 --> 00:02:14,573
puffy face, bird legs.

44
00:02:14,573 --> 00:02:17,827
Many say their head feels full, like it's stuffed.

45
00:02:18,667 --> 00:02:21,187
There's also a good chance you feel sick.

46
00:02:21,187 --> 00:02:25,921
Roughly sixty to eighty percent of space travelers get space motion sickness.

47
00:02:26,421 --> 00:02:30,389
Your inner ear still expects gravity to tell it which way is down.

48
00:02:30,389 --> 00:02:32,193
Your eyes say you're floating.

49
00:02:32,193 --> 00:02:34,358
Your brain can't make the two agree.

50
00:02:34,858 --> 00:02:37,167
A typical bout lasts two or three days.

51
00:02:37,167 --> 00:02:41,491
Then your brain adapts, and down becomes wherever your feet happen to be.

52
00:02:44,233 --> 00:02:47,295
By the end of the first week, your muscles have noticed too.

53
00:02:47,795 --> 00:02:53,051
On Earth, your calves, thighs, back and neck fight gravity every second you stand.

54
00:02:53,051 --> 00:02:55,679
Scientists call them antigravity muscles.

55
00:02:55,679 --> 00:02:57,986
Up here, they have nothing to fight.

56
00:02:58,486 --> 00:03:07,019
The Canadian Space Agency says astronauts can lose up to twenty percent of their muscle mass on flights lasting just five to eleven days.

57
00:03:07,519 --> 00:03:12,877
That's why astronauts on the space station exercise about two and a half hours a day.

58
00:03:12,877 --> 00:03:14,516
In our what-if, so do you.

59
00:03:14,516 --> 00:03:16,091
Every day, for ten years.

60
00:03:18,833 --> 00:03:21,565
One month in, a quieter loss is adding up.

61
00:03:21,565 --> 00:03:22,280
Your bones.

62
00:03:22,780 --> 00:03:23,656
Bone is alive.

63
00:03:23,656 --> 00:03:27,219
It's constantly rebuilt to match the loads you put on it.

64
00:03:27,219 --> 00:03:30,907
Take the load away, and your body starts taking bone apart.

65
00:03:31,407 --> 00:03:34,324
NASA says that on missions of four to six months,

66
00:03:34,324 --> 00:03:40,811
weight-bearing bones like the spine and hips lose one to one and a half percent of their density every month.

67
00:03:41,311 --> 00:03:43,375
That's fast enough, NASA warns,

68
00:03:43,375 --> 00:03:47,770
to put astronauts at risk of fractures and premature osteoporosis,

69
00:03:47,770 --> 00:03:51,698
the bone-thinning disease that normally comes with old age.

70
00:03:52,533 --> 00:03:54,686
But you also get a strange bonus.

71
00:03:54,686 --> 00:03:55,273
You grow.

72
00:03:55,773 --> 00:03:58,081
Without gravity squashing your spine,

73
00:03:58,081 --> 00:04:01,762
the soft discs between your vertebrae relax and spread out.

74
00:04:01,762 --> 00:04:05,941
NASA scientists say astronauts can grow up to three percent taller.

75
00:04:06,441 --> 00:04:10,388
For someone 1.8 meters tall, that's about five centimeters.

76
00:04:10,888 --> 00:04:12,359
It isn't all good news.

77
00:04:12,359 --> 00:04:15,683
In one study of six astronauts on the space station,

78
00:04:15,683 --> 00:04:17,856
more than half reported back pain,

79
00:04:17,856 --> 00:04:22,267
and the muscles that support the lower spine shrank by about a fifth.

80
00:04:25,033 --> 00:04:29,272
Six months in, the doctors watching you pay close attention to your eyes.

81
00:04:29,772 --> 00:04:33,734
That fluid pushed toward your head never fully drains away.

82
00:04:33,734 --> 00:04:39,912
About seventy percent of astronauts on long missions develop signs of something called SANS.

83
00:04:39,912 --> 00:04:42,934
Spaceflight associated neuro-ocular syndrome.

84
00:04:43,434 --> 00:04:45,959
The nerve at the back of the eye can swell.

85
00:04:45,959 --> 00:04:48,073
The back of the eyeball can flatten.

86
00:04:48,073 --> 00:04:50,773
Some astronauts come home needing new glasses.

87
00:04:51,273 --> 00:04:54,661
So far, no astronaut has lost their sight permanently.

88
00:04:54,661 --> 00:04:57,421
But nobody fully understands what causes it,

89
00:04:57,421 --> 00:04:59,930
and longer missions could make it worse.

90
00:05:00,767 --> 00:05:01,482
Quick pause.

91
00:05:01,482 --> 00:05:03,746
If this is blowing your mind a little,

92
00:05:03,746 --> 00:05:06,607
a like and a subscribe really helps the channel.

93
00:05:07,107 --> 00:05:09,505
Now the danger you can't feel at all.

94
00:05:09,505 --> 00:05:10,153
Radiation.

95
00:05:10,653 --> 00:05:14,457
Down here, the atmosphere and Earth's magnetic field shield you.

96
00:05:14,457 --> 00:05:18,975
On the ground, people typically get about two to three millisieverts a year.

97
00:05:19,475 --> 00:05:23,755
In low orbit, you're still partly inside that magnetic shield,

98
00:05:23,755 --> 00:05:26,930
but the station's walls can't stop everything.

99
00:05:26,930 --> 00:05:32,867
Space station crews typically pick up about 0.2 to 0.5 millisieverts every single day.

100
00:05:33,367 --> 00:05:38,635
By our rough math, that's a month or two of ground-level radiation, every day.

101
00:05:38,635 --> 00:05:42,215
NASA says one millisievert is about ten chest X-rays.

102
00:05:42,215 --> 00:05:46,943
So that's like a few chest X-rays, every day, for as long as you stay.

103
00:05:49,700 --> 00:05:50,229
One year.

104
00:05:50,229 --> 00:05:52,464
This is where the real data gets thin.

105
00:05:52,464 --> 00:05:54,463
Very few people have ever done it.

106
00:05:54,963 --> 00:05:56,216
From 2015 to 2016,

107
00:05:56,216 --> 00:06:01,996
NASA astronaut Scott Kelly spent three hundred and forty days on the space station,

108
00:06:01,996 --> 00:06:05,756
while his identical twin brother Mark stayed on Earth.

109
00:06:05,756 --> 00:06:08,054
Ten research teams compared them.

110
00:06:08,554 --> 00:06:09,843
The strangest result?

111
00:06:09,843 --> 00:06:14,140
Scott's telomeres, the protective caps on the ends of his chromosomes,

112
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:15,367
got longer in space.

113
00:06:15,367 --> 00:06:18,743
Within about two days of landing, they shortened again,

114
00:06:18,743 --> 00:06:21,444
and some ended up a bit shorter than before.

115
00:06:21,944 --> 00:06:23,842
His gene activity changed too.

116
00:06:23,842 --> 00:06:28,904
About ninety-one percent of those changes went back to normal within six months.

117
00:06:28,904 --> 00:06:32,574
But his thinking speed and accuracy dropped after landing,

118
00:06:32,574 --> 00:06:34,979
and were still lower six months later.

119
00:06:35,479 --> 00:06:37,827
And remember, it's a study of two people.

120
00:06:37,827 --> 00:06:41,892
The researchers themselves warn that you can't draw firm rules from it.

121
00:06:42,733 --> 00:06:50,172
The record for the longest single stay belongs to Russian doctor and cosmonaut Valeri Polyakov.

122
00:06:50,172 --> 00:06:54,713
Four hundred and thirty-seven days aboard the Mir station,

123
00:06:54,713 --> 00:06:57,218
from January 1994 to March 1995.

124
00:06:57,718 --> 00:07:04,150
His mission was meant to test whether a human body could survive a trip as long as a journey to Mars.

125
00:07:04,150 --> 00:07:08,226
After landing, cosmonauts were usually carried from the capsule.

126
00:07:08,226 --> 00:07:09,308
He chose to walk.

127
00:07:09,808 --> 00:07:14,553
The record for the most total time in space belongs to Oleg Kononenko.

128
00:07:14,553 --> 00:07:19,094
In June 2024, he became the first person to pass one thousand days.

129
00:07:19,094 --> 00:07:23,974
He finished with one thousand one hundred and eleven, over five flights.

130
00:07:24,474 --> 00:07:26,814
Now put our what-if on the same chart.

131
00:07:26,814 --> 00:07:30,571
Ten years is about three thousand six hundred and fifty days.

132
00:07:30,571 --> 00:07:35,128
More than three times anyone has ever spent up there, even added together.

133
00:07:37,867 --> 00:07:38,792
So, ten years.

134
00:07:38,792 --> 00:07:42,493
Nobody has done it, so from here on we're extrapolating.

135
00:07:42,493 --> 00:07:45,203
Treat this as rough math, not a forecast.

136
00:07:45,703 --> 00:07:47,116
Start with radiation.

137
00:07:47,116 --> 00:07:52,164
NASA's limit for an astronaut's entire career is six hundred millisieverts.

138
00:07:52,664 --> 00:07:54,802
At 0.2 to 0.5 millisieverts a day,

139
00:07:54,802 --> 00:07:59,329
you'd reach that limit somewhere between about three and eight years in.

140
00:07:59,329 --> 00:08:04,422
After ten years, you'd have roughly seven hundred and thirty to eighteen hundred.

141
00:08:04,422 --> 00:08:06,623
Up to three times the career limit.

142
00:08:07,123 --> 00:08:07,753
Then bone.

143
00:08:07,753 --> 00:08:11,346
Nobody knows if bone keeps disappearing at the same pace,

144
00:08:11,346 --> 00:08:14,498
because nobody has stayed long enough to find out.

145
00:08:14,498 --> 00:08:18,280
NASA itself says more research is needed on longer missions.

146
00:08:18,780 --> 00:08:19,639
And your eyes,

147
00:08:19,639 --> 00:08:25,655
your arteries and your immune system are all on the list of things that changed in the twin study.

148
00:08:25,655 --> 00:08:27,865
For ten years, the honest answer is:

149
00:08:27,865 --> 00:08:28,725
we don't know.

150
00:08:29,567 --> 00:08:32,350
And that's exactly why space agencies care.

151
00:08:32,350 --> 00:08:35,263
Because a trip to Mars is the real long haul.

152
00:08:35,763 --> 00:08:37,393
In one NASA reference plan,

153
00:08:37,393 --> 00:08:40,714
the flight out takes about one hundred and eighty days,

154
00:08:40,714 --> 00:08:43,914
then you stay about five hundred days on the surface,

155
00:08:43,914 --> 00:08:46,872
and fly another one hundred and eighty days home.

156
00:08:46,872 --> 00:08:48,563
Nearly two and a half years.

157
00:08:49,063 --> 00:08:53,773
On its way to Mars, NASA's Curiosity rover carried a radiation detector.

158
00:08:53,773 --> 00:08:57,437
It measured about 1.8 millisieverts a day in deep space.

159
00:08:57,437 --> 00:08:58,484
By that measure,

160
00:08:58,484 --> 00:09:04,372
just the flights there and back would add up to about six hundred and sixty millisieverts.

161
00:09:04,372 --> 00:09:08,494
Already past the career limit, before anyone sets foot on Mars.

162
00:09:08,994 --> 00:09:11,731
That's what a year on Tiangong is really for.

163
00:09:11,731 --> 00:09:17,085
Every month someone spends up there teaches us how to keep a body working far from home.

164
00:09:17,933 --> 00:09:19,196
And here's the twist.

165
00:09:19,196 --> 00:09:22,382
The ten years in orbit might not be the hardest part.

166
00:09:22,382 --> 00:09:23,284
Coming home is.

167
00:09:23,784 --> 00:09:25,959
Remember those extra five centimeters?

168
00:09:25,959 --> 00:09:26,761
You lose them.

169
00:09:26,761 --> 00:09:31,397
Within a few months back on Earth, astronauts shrink back to their normal height.

170
00:09:31,897 --> 00:09:34,053
A lot of the other changes fade too.

171
00:09:34,053 --> 00:09:35,130
In the twin study,

172
00:09:35,130 --> 00:09:39,441
Scott Kelly's gut bacteria went back to how they were before the flight,

173
00:09:39,441 --> 00:09:42,374
and most of his gene changes went back to normal.

174
00:09:42,874 --> 00:09:44,126
But not everything.

175
00:09:44,126 --> 00:09:46,430
In a study of seventeen astronauts,

176
00:09:46,430 --> 00:09:52,226
the weight-bearing bones of most of them had only partly recovered a year after landing.

177
00:09:52,226 --> 00:09:56,440
The lasting loss was about the same as a decade of normal aging.

178
00:09:56,940 --> 00:09:59,115
And in that study of six astronauts,

179
00:09:59,115 --> 00:10:04,491
the risk of a slipped disc was about four times higher in the months after they got home.

180
00:10:04,491 --> 00:10:08,356
Your spine stretched out in space, and gravity squeezes it back.

181
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:12,383
So ten years in space would stretch you, puff you up,

182
00:10:12,383 --> 00:10:14,906
thin your bones and soak you in radiation.

183
00:10:14,906 --> 00:10:16,227
Much of it would heal.

184
00:10:16,227 --> 00:10:17,068
Not all of it.

185
00:10:17,568 --> 00:10:18,356
And right now,

186
00:10:18,356 --> 00:10:23,086
somebody up there is starting the year that helps us find out how far a body can go.

187
00:10:23,086 --> 00:10:25,339
Would you sign up for the ten-year trip?

188
00:10:25,339 --> 00:10:26,690
Tell me in the comments.
